(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-cms-exam-2-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 2 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 47-year-old surfer has a pterygium that has advanced 2 mm onto the cornea over two years and now induces astigmatism. What is the appropriate management?","choices":["Prescribe glasses to correct the astigmatism","Continue lubricants and sun protection alone","Start topical corticosteroids long term","Reassure, as pterygia never affect vision","Refer for surgical excision"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Surgery is indicated when it grows onto the cornea and distorts vision. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 28"},{"q":"What underlies dacryocystitis?","choices":["Meibomian gland obstruction","Corneal epithelial loss","Trabecular meshwork obstruction","Nasolacrimal duct obstruction","Lacrimal gland inflammation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman is being treated for dacryocystitis. A mass is noted ABOVE the medial canthal tendon rather than below it. What does that suggest?","choices":["Simple nasolacrimal duct obstruction","Pre-septal cellulitis","Dacryoadenitis","A chalazion of the upper lid","A lacrimal sac tumour"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A mass above the tendon rather than below it raises that possibility. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man presents 40 minutes after sudden painless monocular vision loss. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure and observe overnight","Prescribe topical antibiotics","Arrange outpatient review within a week","Immediate ophthalmology referral","Start oral corticosteroids and review in a month"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is a stroke of the eye — the window is measured in minutes to hours. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man is found to have open-angle glaucoma. Where is the abnormality?","choices":["The lens pushing the iris forward","The ciliary body producing excess aqueous","The iris blocking the drainage angle","The trabecular meshwork by the canal of Schlemm","The optic nerve head itself"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — an age-related outflow abnormality. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which features describe a benign iris nevus?","choices":["Flat, under 3 mm and on the inferior iris","Associated with discharge","Painful and photophobic","Bilateral and symmetrical","Raised, vascular and rapidly growing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and usually apparent around puberty. Avascular as well. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman has macular degeneration. Which part of her vision is preserved?","choices":["Fine detail at distance","Colour vision only","The peripheral field","The central field","Reading vision"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — central vision is lost while navigational vision remains. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man with recurrent anterior uveitis is being investigated. His back pain improves with exercise and worsens with rest. Which association is most likely?","choices":["HLA-B27 spondyloarthropathy","Chronic sun and wind exposure","Contact lens overwear","Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Hypertension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the ankylosing spondylitis association, which is why the systemic history matters. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man with a hordeolum has developed spreading lid erythema and oedema with fever. How does this change management?","choices":["Incise and drain immediately","Refer for elective excision","Start topical antibiotics only","Treat on the cellulitis pathway","Continue warm compresses alone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — pre-septal cellulitis alongside a hordeolum is managed as cellulitis. With systemic antibiotics. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has sudden painless monocular vision loss. Which systemic workup does the diagnosis trigger?","choices":["A dermatological review","Allergy testing","Pulmonary function testing","A stroke workup with carotid imaging","Thyroid function testing alone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is a stroke of the eye and carries the same embolic sources. Cardiac assessment as well. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 40"},{"q":"Besides accidental trauma, which causes of periorbital haematoma are named?","choices":["Allergic conjunctivitis","Chronic sun exposure","Orbital surgery, injections and varices","Contact lens wear","Corticosteroid drops"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it is not always traumatic. Eyelid surgery, peribulbar injections and lymphangiomas too. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which pair of neuro-ophthalmological conditions produces ptosis?","choices":["Adie tonic pupil and Argyll Robertson pupil","Relative afferent pupillary defect alone","Fourth and sixth nerve palsies","Nystagmus and anisocoria","Horner syndrome and third nerve palsy"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — both drop the lid, and each also disturbs the pupil, so ptosis appears alongside anisocoria. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 2"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man reports that over the past week straight lines have become sharply distorted in the right eye and a grey patch has appeared in the centre of his vision. Fundoscopy shows subretinal fluid and haemorrhage at the macula. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Prescribe high-dose antioxidant supplements alone","Urgent referral for intravitreal anti-VEGF therapy","Start topical corticosteroids","Reassure and review in six months","Refer for cataract surgery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The wet form is treatable and the window matters. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman develops sudden ptosis and miosis on the left with neck pain after a whiplash injury. What must be excluded urgently?","choices":["Diabetic third nerve palsy","Multiple sclerosis","Carotid artery dissection","Apical lung tumour","Giant cell arteritis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Painful Horner syndrome of sudden onset is a dissection until proven otherwise. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 5-week-old infant is found to have an absent red reflex in the left eye at the newborn check. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure, as red reflex varies in newborns","Prescribe patching of the right eye","Review at the six-month check","Urgent ophthalmology referral","Arrange a hearing test"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An absent red reflex demands urgent assessment — cataract and retinoblastoma both present this way. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy was struck in the eye with a ball. He has severe pain, nausea and vomiting, and bradycardia on attempted upgaze, but the eye looks remarkably quiet. What does this suggest?","choices":["Concussion without ocular injury","Periorbital haematoma","Simple periorbital contusion","Inferior rectus entrapment in a floor fracture","Anterior uveitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Restricted upgaze with severe pain and AUTONOMIC DISTURBANCE — bradycardia and vomiting on attempted movement — is the entrapment picture. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman with red, crusted lid margins and a frothy tear film has used lid hygiene twice daily for two weeks with no improvement. What is the next step?","choices":["Start oral antibiotics immediately","Continue lid hygiene alone for three months","Add a topical antibiotic","Refer for lid surgery","Start topical corticosteroid alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lid hygiene first; no better after two weeks, escalate to topical, then oral antibiotics. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 19"},{"q":"How does branch retinal artery occlusion differ from the central form?","choices":["It requires different treatment","A branch is blocked, so only part is affected","It is painful where the central form is painless","It affects both eyes","It resolves spontaneously"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the field loss is partial and localised, and management does not change. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man is having fluorescein angiography. How quickly does the dye reach the eye after injection, and what must be checked first?","choices":["Two minutes, and iodine allergy must be excluded","Ten to fifteen seconds, and it contains no iodine","Thirty minutes, and thyroid function must be checked","Immediately, and it requires general anaesthesia","One hour, and renal function must be checked"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the absence of iodine matters for patients who report contrast allergy. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which of these ophthalmological presentations is EMERGENT?","choices":["Xanthelasma","Gonococcal conjunctivitis in a newborn","A resolving subconjunctival haemorrhage","Dermatochalasis with heaviness","Pinguecula with mild irritation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — untreated it risks corneal perforation. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man with high myopia is counselled about long-term risks. Which is he at increased risk of?","choices":["Trachoma","Retinoblastoma","Hyperopia in later life","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Retinal detachment"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — the longer eye has a thinner, more vulnerable peripheral retina. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 18"},{"q":"How does branch retinal vein occlusion differ from the central form?","choices":["The clot is in a branch vein","It is painful","It affects the arterial supply","It causes total vision loss","It needs no referral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — haemorrhage is confined to that territory. Only part of the retina is affected. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has horizontal double vision that is worse when looking to the left, with the left eye failing to abduct. Which muscle is weak?","choices":["The superior oblique","The medial rectus","The levator palpebrae","The lateral rectus","The inferior rectus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — supplied by the sixth cranial nerve, which abducts the eye. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 48-year-old patient has acute blurring of vision and severe pain in the left eye that began 30 minutes ago, with halos around lights. They are nauseated and have vomited. The pain started while they were relaxing on their porch. Temperature 36.9°C, pulse 90/min, blood pressure 130/90 mm Hg, respirations 20/min. Examination reveals a shallow anterior chamber, a hazy cornea, a fixed moderately dilated pupil and ciliary injection. What is the most appropriate next step in managing this patient?","choices":["Tonometry","Discharge with topical antibiotic eye drops","X-ray to rule out a foreign body","Topical atropine to facilitate ophthalmoscopy","Lumbar puncture"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Measuring the intraocular pressure confirms the diagnosis and is the immediate step. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which mechanisms cause a full-thickness eye wall laceration?","choices":["A fingernail","Blunt force from a fist or ball","A sharp object or high-velocity projectile","Sun and wind exposure","Contact lens overwear"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and the object may have been withdrawn before arrival. A fishing hook or knife, for example. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has noticed one pupil is larger than the other. The larger pupil reacts poorly to light but constricts slowly on sustained near effort, then redilates slowly. Deep tendon reflexes are diminished. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Adie tonic pupil","Argyll Robertson pupil","Pharmacological mydriasis","Horner syndrome","Third nerve palsy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A large tonic pupil with a slow near response; diminished reflexes make it Holmes-Adie syndrome. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which lens corrects astigmatism?","choices":["A toric lens","A bifocal lens","A prism","A concave lens","A convex lens"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 3-year-old boy is unbothered when his left eye is covered, but becomes fussy when his right eye is covered. What does this test show?","choices":["Neither eye is amblyopic","He has a sixth nerve palsy","The left eye is amblyopic","Both eyes are equally affected","The right eye is amblyopic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The occlusion objection test: the child objects when the GOOD eye is covered. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man sustains a globe rupture from an airbag. Where do such ruptures commonly occur?","choices":["At the optic nerve head","Behind the muscle insertions, superonasally","In the posterior pole","At the centre of the cornea","At the limbus only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and at sites of previous surgery. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with giant cell arteritis and vision loss in one eye asks why he must start treatment today rather than wait for the biopsy. What is the reason?","choices":["The biopsy becomes negative after treatment starts","The erythrocyte sedimentation rate will normalise and obscure the diagnosis","Steroids are only effective in the first hour","The vision in the affected eye will return","The fellow eye can be lost within days"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which mechanism produces the Adie tonic pupil?","choices":["Compression of the third nerve by an aneurysm","Ciliary ganglion damage, then reinnervation","A sympathetic chain lesion","Demyelination of the optic nerve","Trabecular meshwork obstruction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Inflammation of the ganglion or short ciliary nerves, then aberrant reinnervation. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has a red, watering right eye that began three days after a head cold. The left eye became involved yesterday. There is no pain. Examination shows diffuse injection with follicles inferiorly, considerable tearing, and a TENDER preauricular node. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Episcleritis","Adult inclusion conjunctivitis","Viral conjunctivitis","Allergic conjunctivitis","Bacterial conjunctivitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Watery discharge, follicles, a tender preauricular node, and one eye then the other after a cold. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 37"},{"q":"When is nystagmus usually asymptomatic?","choices":["Never; it is always symptomatic","When it is horizontal","When it is acquired in adulthood","When it is bilateral","When it is acquired before age eight"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — otherwise it is usually symptomatic. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman has severe deep eye pain that wakes her at night, with a bluish hue to the sclera visible in daylight. What systemic evaluation is warranted?","choices":["Allergy skin testing","An autoimmune workup","Thyroid function alone","Carotid Doppler studies","Sexual health screening"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — scleritis is frequently associated with systemic autoimmune disease. For connective tissue disease. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 10-year-old boy is diagnosed with amblyopia for the first time. Why does his age matter?","choices":["It will resolve spontaneously by adolescence","Treatment works less well as maturity approaches","Glasses cannot be prescribed at this age","Amblyopia only occurs after age ten","Surgery becomes the only option at ten"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the earlier it is treated, the better the outcome. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman complains of a constant foreign body sensation in the right eye. Examination shows the lower lid margin turned inward, with the lashes resting against the globe, and conjunctival injection. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Entropion","Trichiasis alone","Blepharitis","Dermatochalasis","Ectropion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lid margin turns IN and the lashes touch the globe, producing the foreign body sensation. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has had a painless, firm nodule in her upper lid for six weeks. Warm compresses and lid massage for a month have not reduced it. Vision is unaffected. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Continue warm compresses for a further six months","Incise and drain it in clinic","Start oral antibiotics","Refer to ophthalmology for steroid injection or curettage","Excise the whole lid lesion under general anaesthetic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Conservative measures come first; a lesion that has not resolved goes on for intralesional steroid or curettage. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which causes of ectropion are named?","choices":["Sun and wind exposure","Ageing and scarring","Chronic steroid drops","Contact lens wear","Diabetes alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and seventh nerve palsy causes ectropion ONLY. Congenital causes too, and seventh nerve palsy. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man has a metallic foreign body embedded in the cornea with a surrounding rust ring, 24 hours after grinding. Vision is 6/9. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Refer for immediate surgical exploration of the globe","Irrigate with saline only","Patch and discharge without removal","Leave it and review in a week","Remove it and the rust ring, then antibiotics"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — the rust ring is removed too, as it perpetuates inflammation. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 21"},{"q":"Besides leukocoria, how may retinoblastoma present?","choices":["Sudden painless total vision loss","A painful red eye with discharge","Poor vision or a turned eye","Bilateral disc swelling","Recurrent nosebleeds"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 41"},{"q":"An 18-year-old woman has had a swelling of her left upper eyelid for 10 days. For the first day or two it was red and mildly painful; it is now painless but has grown. There has been no drainage, no change in vision and no itching. She has been well otherwise. Vital signs are normal, extraocular movements are intact and the pupils are equal and reactive. The left upper lid carries a 1.5 cm round, non-tender, mildly erythematous mass with no drainage. The underside of the lid is greyish-red. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hordeolum","Dacryoadenitis","Chalazion","Blepharitis","Dacryocystitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A sterile obstruction of a meibomian gland: it builds over days to weeks and the mature lesion is NON-tender, which is what the painless second week tells you. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man with a red painful eye is found to have keratitis. Which examination step is essential before any treatment is chosen?","choices":["Fluorescein at the slit lamp","Colour vision testing","Tonometry alone","Dilated fundus examination","Visual field testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it distinguishes a dendrite from an ulcer from punctate disease, and each is treated differently. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man has non-arteritic ischaemic optic neuropathy. What is the management?","choices":["Observation and risk factor modification","Intravitreal injection","Urgent temporal artery biopsy and steroids","High-dose intravenous corticosteroids","Immediate surgery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with consideration of avoiding antihypertensives at bedtime. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 52"},{"q":"Why does astigmatism blur vision at every distance?","choices":["Uneven curvature, so no single focus","The eye is too short","The lens is opaque","The eye is too long","The lens has stiffened"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — multiple focal points. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman has painless loss of part of her upper visual field. Fundoscopy shows haemorrhages confined to a wedge of retina below the macula, stopping at the horizontal raphe. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Retinal detachment","Branch retinal vein occlusion","Optic neuritis","Central retinal vein occlusion","Branch retinal artery occlusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The changes respect the territory of a single branch vein. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman who sleeps in her contact lenses has a painful red right eye with a white spot on the cornea and an overlying epithelial defect that stains with fluorescein. Which organism is of greatest concern?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus","Chlamydia trachomatis","Moraxella catarrhalis","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Adenovirus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The contact lens association makes this the organism to fear, and it can perforate rapidly. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has a lid laceration through the lid MARGIN of the upper lid. Why does that require specialist repair?","choices":["It always transects the canaliculus","It always involves the levator muscle","It cannot be repaired at all","It indicates an open globe","A poor repair leaves a permanent notch"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — margin-involving lacerations need meticulous approximation. And chronic irritation. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 17-year-old boy with a traumatic hyphema is being counselled. Which complication is he being monitored for over the next few days?","choices":["Rebleeding with a rise in intraocular pressure","Retinal detachment within 24 hours","Corneal ulceration","Optic neuritis","Development of a cataract within 48 hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the rebleed risk peaks in the first three to five days. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 77-year-old woman has sudden painless vision loss with a NEW temporal headache, scalp tenderness and jaw claudication. Which investigations rule the diagnosis in or out?","choices":["Magnetic resonance imaging of brain and orbits","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein","Carotid Doppler and echocardiography","Ocular ultrasound","Gonioscopy and tonometry"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with temporal artery biopsy as the gold standard. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman has anisocoria that is GREATER in dim light. Which condition does this suggest?","choices":["Horner syndrome","Adie tonic pupil","Pharmacological mydriasis","Argyll Robertson pupil","Third nerve palsy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the sympathetically denervated pupil fails to dilate, so the difference grows in the dark. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman has a suspected globe rupture. What is the immediate management?","choices":["Pad and bandage the eye firmly","Irrigate the eye copiously","Discharge with oral antibiotics","Instil topical anaesthetic and examine under pressure","Rigid shield, nil by mouth, urgent referral"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Nothing that raises intraocular pressure, and no pressure on the eye. Antiemetics as well, to stop straining. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has a confirmed open globe. Besides the shield and the ophthalmology call, why are antiemetics and analgesia given?","choices":["To speed corneal healing","To allow examination without anaesthetic","To lower the intraocular pressure directly","So the patient does not strain","To reduce the risk of infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — straining raises pressure on an open eye. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man with anterior uveitis is prescribed a cycloplegic alongside the steroid. What is its purpose?","choices":["To relieve pain and prevent synechiae","To treat the underlying infection","To improve near vision","To lower the intraocular pressure","To reduce corneal oedema"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it rests the ciliary body and keeps the iris from adhering to the lens. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman with open-angle glaucoma is started on drops. Which agents are named first-line?","choices":["Atropine or cyclopentolate","Acyclovir ointment","Latanoprost, tafluprost or timolol","Olopatadine","Prednisolone drops"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man with a corneal dendrite is prescribed treatment. Which agent must be AVOIDED?","choices":["Oral antiviral","Topical antiviral","Topical corticosteroid","Lubricating drops","Cycloplegic for comfort"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Steroid on an active dendritic ulcer can cause it to enlarge into a geographic ulcer. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man reports flashes and floaters but no field loss. Examination shows a posterior vitreous detachment with no retinal break. What is the appropriate advice?","choices":["Start topical steroids","No follow-up is needed","Patch the eye","Bed rest for two weeks","Return if a curtain or shadow appears"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — the warning is what converts this from observation to emergency. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man is brought in after a workshop explosion. His right eye is obviously injured and he is breathing noisily. What comes first?","choices":["A rigid shield over the injured eye","Airway, breathing and circulation","Ophthalmology consultation","Visual acuity in both eyes","Computed tomography of the orbits"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The eye waits until the patient is stable. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 6-year-old girl has 6/6 vision in the right eye and 6/24 in the left, which does not improve with glasses. The eyes are straight and the fundi are normal. What is the treatment principle?","choices":["Operate to align the eyes","Prescribe glasses alone and review in a year","Patch the weaker eye to rest it","Patch the BETTER eye to force use of the weaker one","Observe until age ten"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — occlusion therapy drives cortical development in the amblyopic eye. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has clear fluid dripping from his nose after a head injury. On the bedsheet it dries as a central red stain with a clear outer ring. What does this indicate?","choices":["Lacrimal duct injury","Allergic rhinorrhoea","Simple epistaxis","Sinus infection","A cerebrospinal fluid leak"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — the halo or double ring sign. Neurosurgical referral and admission. From a basilar skull fracture. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man reports a grey shadow in his peripheral vision that changes as he moves his head. What does that positional change indicate?","choices":["Optic neuritis","A vitreous haemorrhage alone","Amaurosis fugax","A retinal detachment","Papilledema"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the deficit shifts with head position. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has spoke-like opacities extending inward from the lens periphery. When do symptoms begin?","choices":["Only after surgery","Once the opacity reaches the centre","Immediately, with loss of distance vision","Only in dim light","Never; it is always asymptomatic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is asymptomatic until then. Glare is the commonest complaint. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man with bacterial conjunctivitis shows no improvement after 24 hours of topical antibiotic. What does that indicate?","choices":["He should stop treatment and observe","He should switch to oral antibiotics himself","It confirms an allergic cause","The course should simply be extended to a week","Referral is warranted"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A prompt and total response is expected in a normal host, so failure at 24 hours changes the plan. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 24-year-old sexually active man has a red, mildly sticky left eye that has persisted for five weeks despite two courses of topical antibiotics. Examination shows large follicles in the lower fornix and a palpable preauricular node. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bacterial conjunctivitis","Viral conjunctivitis","Adult inclusion conjunctivitis","Allergic conjunctivitis","Blepharitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chlamydial disease is chronic and follicular, and it does not respond to ordinary topical antibiotics. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 14-month-old boy is brought in because his mother noticed a white glow in his right pupil in photographs. There is no redness or discharge. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Congenital cataract","Conjunctivitis","Strabismus alone","Coats disease","Retinoblastoma"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Leukocoria in a young child is retinoblastoma until proven otherwise. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man has itchy, watery, red eyes every spring. Both eyes are affected. Examination shows diffuse injection, chemosis and conjunctival papillae, with no preauricular node. Vision is normal. What is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Topical corticosteroids as first line","Topical antibiotic drops","An antihistamine and mast cell stabiliser","Artificial tears alone","Oral acyclovir"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — olopatadine does both. With allergen avoidance, cool compresses and artificial tears. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 35"}]};
